                "senderName": "NWS Des Moines IA",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 8 at 6:29PM CDT until September 9 at 12:30AM CDT by NWS Des Moines IA",
                "description": "FFWDMX\n\nThe National Weather Service in Des Moines has issued a\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nEastern Dallas County in central Iowa...\nPolk County in central Iowa...\n\n* Until 1230 AM CDT Wednesday.\n\n* At 629 PM CDT, trained weather spotters reported thunderstorms\nproducing heavy rain across the warned area. Between 1 and 2 inches\nof rain have fallen. Additional rainfall amounts of 1 to 2 inches\nare possible in the warned area. Flash flooding is already\noccurring.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Trained spotters reported.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nDes Moines, West Des Moines, Ankeny, Urbandale, Johnston, Clive,\nAltoona, Pleasant Hill, Grimes, Windsor Heights, Carlisle,\nBondurant, Polk City, Saylorville, Mitchellville, Adventureland\nAmusement Park, Iowa State Fairgrounds and Prairie Meadows.\n\nThis includes the following highways...\nInterstate 80 between mile markers 121 and 149. Interstate 235 near\nmile marker 1...and between mile markers 10 and 14...and between mile\nmarkers 2 and 9. Interstate 35 between mile markers 100 and 102...and\nbetween mile markers 68 and 99.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nFor the latest stream observations and forecasts refer to\nweather.gov/desmoines/water.",
                "response": "Avoid",
